<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#parliamentary-democracy">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parliamentary democracy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1721 CE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parliamentary democracy is government by ministers drawn from and answerable to an elected parliament, which can remove them by withdrawing its confidence. Growing out of 18th-century British cabinet government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Robert Walpole</a> and spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, it now prevails in most of Europe and the Commonwealth, praised for flexibility and accountability and criticised for unstable coalitions and weak checks.</p></div>
